  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe forecast area relishes within high pressure parked to the\nsouth today, yielding dry weather, mostly clear skies, and\nwarming temperatures.\n\nFor early this morning, river valley fog present across the\nsouthern coalfields and up the Appalachians will continue to\ndance through the sheltered valleys heading into the predawn\nhours. The VAD wind profile shows slightly breezier winds parked\nright above the surface, which will cause fog to move about the\nvalleys and cause periodic visibility restrictions. Will\ncontinue to monitor overnight trends to determine whether an SPS\nis warranted for the morning commute.\n\nOtherwise, fog erosion after daybreak will branch out into\nanother dry and pleasant day under the guise of high pressure\nanchored over the southeast US. Breezy southerly winds will\nencourage warm air advection to impose afternoon temperatures to\nreach above their climatological norms for this time of year.\nMaximum temps spanning the low 80s across the lowlands and the\nupper 60s to mid 70s are expected for today, but looks to fall\nshort of exceeding any records.\n\nWhile the surface high begins to lose influence over the area on\nWednesday, the bulk of the day looks to remain dry and warm\nbefore unsettled weather returns with a cold front.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe brief period of dry weather comes to an end beginning\nWednesday afternoon across southeast Ohio as a cold front\npresses down from the north. Unseasonably warm temperatures\nexpected in the forecast area ahead of the frontal passage will\nbolster enough instability suitable of strong thunderstorm \ndevelopment along the boundary. A strengthening mid-level wind\nfield will support damaging wind gusts within any strong storm\nthat forms in the area Wednesday afternoon and evening.\nAdditionally, localized high water issues could transpire within\nheavy downpours accompanying the front's southward progression.\nA Level 1 (marginal) risk was introduced with the day 2 outlooks\nfrom both WPC and SPC on Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe remainder of the work week will remain unsettled as the\naforementioned cold front is anticipated to stall across the\nTennessee Valley and retreat north as a warm front. This will\nmaintain at least low end chances of showers and storms Thursday\ninto Friday morning ahead of a secondary cold front slated for\nthe later half of Friday. In the wake of this frontal passage, a\nblast of Arctic air will invade from the north and will impose a\nwidespread frost/freeze heading into Saturday morning. \nAdditionally, lingering moisture along the ridges of the higher \nterrain could see a brief changeover to snow. While daytime \ntemperatures recover to the 40s and 50s Saturday afternoon, \nanother night of sub-freezing conditions look plausible Saturday\nnight into Sunday morning.",
